        "faq_1": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What temperature should I use for green tea?",
            "answer": "<p>Green tea is best brewed around 170–180°F. Boiling water at 212°F scorches the delicate leaves and makes them bitter, so a temperature-control kettle lets you dial in the lower heat that keeps green tea smooth and sweet.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_2": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I really need a temperature-control kettle?",
            "answer": "<p>If you only drink black tea, instant coffee, or just need hot water fast, a Fast-Boil kettle is all you need. But if you enjoy green, white, or oolong tea — or brew pour-over coffee — temperature control makes a real difference in flavor, since those drinks taste best below a full boil.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_3": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What's the difference between preset and advanced digital temperature-control kettles?",
            "answer": "<p>Preset kettles let you pick from 5 preset temperatures matched to different teas. Advanced digital models step up to as many as 7 presets, add a steep timer for perfect brewing, and include a keep-warm function that holds your water hot for up to an hour.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_4": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I still boil water all the way to 212°F?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Temperature-control kettles include a full-boil option alongside their lower presets, so you can brew black or herbal tea, make instant coffee, or cook ramen and oatmeal at 212°F, then switch to a gentler setting for green or white tea.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_5": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What temperature is best for pour-over coffee?",
            "answer": "<p>Pour-over and French press coffee brew best around 195–205°F, just below boiling. A temperature-control kettle lets you hit that window consistently for a cleaner, more even extraction.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_6": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How do I clean and descale a temperature-control kettle?",
            "answer": "<p>If you have hard water, descale monthly: fill the kettle with a 50/50 mix of water and white vinegar, boil, let it sit 15–20 minutes, then rinse well. Hand-wash the kettle body and any infuser basket, and never submerge the electrical base.</p>"
          }
        },
